= The Munich beer hall

On the evening of 8 November, Adolf Hitler and the NSDAP's armed squads occupy the Bürgerbräukeller, where Kahr is speaking, and claim to have overthrown the governments in Munich and Berlin. General Ludendorff joins them. The next day the Bavarian police open fire on their march at the Feldherrnhalle. Sixteen Nazis (some sources say fifteen), four policemen and a bystander are dead, and Hitler is arrested.

Pay attention to what comes next. The state of Bavaria, whose commissioner Kahr had planned a march on Berlin himself and abandoned it, will let Hitler stand trial in Munich and make speeches. The judges will see a patriot. The NSDAP will be banned, Ludendorff acquitted, and Hitler sentenced to five years of fortress confinement, of which he will serve less than nine months. He will write a book in a cell.

The republic's institutions, run by the DNVP's friends in the bench and by the Centre and DVP's friends in Bavaria's government, treat a coup from the Right as an excess of enthusiasm, and a rising from the Left as a crime. The same Reichswehr that marched into Saxony in a week has not marched into Bavaria in a year.

